NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"indexeddb-donor-coverage-port","children":[{"type":"text","value":"IndexedDB donor coverage port"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed implementation: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c4441632c7ac595d11c57061f5c5f9a3ae556de9"},{"type":"text","value":".\nProduction baseline: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fd3c05c818d76489438224e24d4e935751596dc0"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThis record concerns DP08, DP09, DP10 and DP17 from the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-06-indexeddb-donor-survey.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sighted donor survey"}]},{"type":"text","value":".\nIt preserves the survey's original dispositions as historical evidence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"result-and-receiving-scope","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result and receiving scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No production changes or new product policy were required. The port extends the\nexisting per-store and persistence models and adds a separate distribution smoke\nlane. The shared rich-value definition is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-clone-oracle.ts"},{"type":"text","value":"; its\ncontrolled receiving companion is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence-values-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". The native\ndriver is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"e2e/coverage-browser.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", with comparisons in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value-oracle.spec.ts"},{"type":"text","value":".\nDrivers are not counted as additional independent models."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Executed law and bound"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limit"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DP08"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two/three competing first native opens; same/independent absent names; complete identical declarations; schema, held restore/readiness, disjoint writes, convergence, fresh restore and later upgrade. Native engines receive three-open histories."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Opens settle before writes; no callback-order promise or union of conflicting declarations. No new liveness guarantee."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DP09"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Seven authored native value shapes: Date, odd ArrayBuffer, offset Uint8Array/DataView, BigInt64Array, Blob and nested array. Unrelated scalar update, peer, export/import, fresh descriptor; insert/import rejection at first/middle/last nested function; raw row/version rollback and valid suffix."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No post-submission mutation, custom prototypes, cycles, Map/Set, detached/shared/resizable buffers or schema transforms in this companion."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DP10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Six legal database/store substitutions, prefix neighbor and anchor; scalar typed keys in separate transactions, update/import/clear, peer/public/durable/restore and untouched version values."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native receiving covers prototype/Unicode creation and ordinary writes, not the entire controlled renaming/import/clear matrix."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DP17"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tarball ESM/CJS consumers plus missing-export controls; Vite with no aliases/config rescue; native browser execution of persist/reopen."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installed dependency closure is pinned with tarball overrides. This does not prove registry range selection, old runtimes, all bundlers or every export."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The new finite grammars intentionally extend existing models without multiplying\nall value shapes by the entire lifecycle state space. Existing fixed/random\nhistory campaigns and direct replay remain unchanged. The TLA+ lifecycle models\nare unchanged because names, payload fidelity and package resolution add no\nlifecycle or authority policy."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sensitivity-and-test-first-evidence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sensitivity and test-first evidence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/review-evidence/donor-port/","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"review-evidence/donor-port"}]},{"type":"text","value":" contains the\nreplay script, source hash, classifications and complete receipts."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name-prefix"},{"type":"text","value":": change exact metadata-store matching to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"startsWith"},{"type":"text","value":".\nSix assertion failures at metadata ownership after replacement; a neighbor's\nentries disappear. This is neither timeout nor setup failure."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"premature-readiness"},{"type":"text","value":": publish readiness before persisted restore.\nFour assertions reject "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ready"},{"type":"text","value":" while native reads remain held/pending."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"json-values"},{"type":"text","value":": JSON-convert accepted rows before native persistence.\nThe Date history rejects the peer's string value at the first insert checkpoint."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two resolver controls remove one installed tarball entry each. ESM/CJS loaders\nfail with the missing-entry path; workspace aliases cannot rescue either path."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value-observer controls accept each correctly constructed corpus value and\nreject flattened shapes, lost view offsets, altered backing bytes and omissions."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All production mutants were restored. These establish sensitivity to the named\nwrong designs, not universal coverage. There is no newly repaired source bug."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first legal-name draft inserted numeric "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" and string "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"0\""},{"type":"text","value":" in one Collection\ntransaction and reproduced the already tracked HC005 core payload collision.\nBoth ordinary and unusual names lost the numeric row. The final name grammar uses\nseparate transactions to isolate routing; HC005 stays open with its existing core\nowner rather than being represented as fixed by this port."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"native-provider-distinction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native provider distinction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chromium and Firefox receive all seven preservation shapes. The local Playwright\nWebKit provider rejects Blob preparation with native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UnknownError: Error preparing Blob/File data to be stored in object store"},{"type":"text","value":". A direct native transaction without\nadapter code reproduces it. The executable raw-provider witness preserves this\nclassification."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the two Blob-containing WebKit cells, the test requires that exact native\nfailure, matching adapter rejection, empty public/durable/export rows, and a\nsuccessful Date suffix. These are executed rejection tests, not skipped tests or\nBlob-preservation evidence. If native Blob storage succeeds on another platform,\nthe same test executes the full preservation history. A Blob-capable WebKit\nreceiving witness is still needed for that claim; the coverage map names it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"guide-audit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide audit"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evidence"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing persistence, schema, readiness and per-store contracts; each owner states its finite domain and exclusions. No recovery/ownership policy is inferred from a donor."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authored per-store arrays and tagged value descriptions never derive expected rows from production storage or version classifiers. Production receives detached scalar inputs or fresh native values."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Opening and adjacent prose distinguish law, value model, finite grammar, real API driver and checkpoint in each oracle/companion."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No new random generator: bounded enumeration is explicit. Removing unusual names loses dictionary/routing cases; removing independent names loses isolation; removing native holds loses premature-readiness reach; removing offsets/types loses fidelity. Empty/reserved stores and conflicting declarations remain separate negative controls."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native open admission counts precede settlement; zero old versions prove absent names; held native reads precede readiness. Public/peer/raw/export/restore observations occur at stated cuts. Package consumers execute real loaders and storage."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three production mutants fail by assertion at the named cuts; value and packed resolver controls remain executable."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered for new finite enumerations. Existing generated persistence and cross-tab campaigns remain registered and unchanged."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No lifecycle states added. Value tags, content, offsets and backing bytes remain because an untouched-field observation distinguishes them; object identity is deliberately outside the law."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection status/readiness, persisted restore, public snapshot, native transaction and settlement retain glossary meanings. Value descriptions are test-only observations, not product states."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled harness retains primary failures and cleanup errors. Native runner aggregates cleanup errors with the primary cause. Tarball fixtures own/dispose temporary directories. No shrinker or failure classifier was changed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native constructors and independent typed/byte observations reject shared JSON assumptions. Direct native Blob admission distinguishes provider rejection from adapter corruption. Legal-name substitution preserves authored meaning."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This versioned record identifies the reviewed implementation and reports each applicable obligation, calibration and open boundary. No universal bug-class closure is claimed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefix neighbor versus exact-name metadata; held versus completed restore; offset versus flattened view; native rejected Blob versus accepted Date; existing versus missing packed export are distinguishing neighbors."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native first opens, held restore and cross-page values run in Chromium/Firefox/WebKit. WebKit Blob preservation and the wider native naming matrix are explicitly unclaimed, with owners in the coverage map."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"validation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validation"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2,446 adapter runtime/type cases passed; 15 files, no type errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"192 native browser cases passed across Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. Of the\n36 added cases, 19 prove rich-value preservation, two prove the documented\nnative Blob rejection/suffix, 12 prove first-open readiness/schema, and three\nexecute packed browser bundles."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"16 package-consumer cases passed: 12 existing declaration checks, two packed\nruntime examples and two missing-export controls."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full test-driver typecheck, changed-file ESLint, formatting and whitespace\nchecks passed. No production code changed, so no core rerun is claimed."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An attempted dependency refresh hit the configured registry's unavailable\npackages. Tests used the available cached Vite/Rollup installation; source,\nmanifest and lockfile were not changed to bypass it. A dependency declaration\nremoved by its prepare script was restored from the local package cache; external\npacking now uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"npm pack --ignore-scripts"},{"type":"text","value":". No suite rebuilds another suite's\ninput. The native CI build list now includes the adapter before packed-browser\nconsumption."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-remains","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What remains"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Product decisions: abnormal native closure (DP02), mutable-value capture and\nownership (DP12), and worker-host support (DP13). These have not been silently\nassigned recovery behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional new APIs: blocked-operation diagnostics, atomic storage read-modify-write,\nand durability hints. Existing accepted contracts remain unchanged."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider evidence: WebKit Blob preservation and the wider native naming matrix."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing open boundaries, including HC005 and the previously recorded lifecycle/\nsuspension/crash limits, remain in the coverage map. This port does not retire them."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"approved-follow-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approved follow-up"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The later "},{"type":"link","href":"2026-10-06-indexeddb-donor-followup.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"follow-up audit"}]},{"type":"text","value":" records the\napproved closure/capture/worker contracts, optional blocked diagnostics, core\ntyped-key repair, and completed native naming and persistent-WebKit Blob evidence.\nThe earlier findings above retain their original revision and evidence boundary."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"indexeddb-donor-coverage-port","text":"IndexedDB donor coverage port","level":1},{"id":"result-and-receiving-scope","text":"Result and receiving scope","level":2},{"id":"sensitivity-and-test-first-evidence","text":"Sensitivity and test-first evidence","level":2},{"id":"native-provider-distinction","text":"Native provider distinction","level":2},{"id":"guide-audit","text":"Guide audit","level":2},{"id":"validation","text":"Validation","level":2},{"id":"what-remains","text":"What remains","level":2},{"id":"approved-follow-up","text":"Approved follow-up","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# IndexedDB donor coverage port\n\nReviewed implementation: `c4441632c7ac595d11c57061f5c5f9a3ae556de9`.\nProduction baseline: `fd3c05c818d76489438224e24d4e935751596dc0`.\nThis record concerns DP08, DP09, DP10 and DP17 from the\n[sighted donor survey](2026-10-06-indexeddb-donor-survey.md).\nIt preserves the survey's original dispositions as historical evidence.\n\n## Result and receiving scope\n\nNo production changes or new product policy were required. The port extends the\nexisting per-store and persistence models and adds a separate distribution smoke\nlane. The shared rich-value definition is `structured-clone-oracle.ts`; its\ncontrolled receiving companion is `persistence-values-oracle.test.ts`. The native\ndriver is `e2e/coverage-browser.ts`, with comparisons in `value-oracle.spec.ts`.\nDrivers are not counted as additional independent models.\n\n| Finding | Executed law and bound | Limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| DP08 | Two/three competing first native opens; same/independent absent names; complete identical declarations; schema, held restore/readiness, disjoint writes, convergence, fresh restore and later upgrade. Native engines receive three-open histories. | Opens settle before writes; no callback-order promise or union of conflicting declarations. No new liveness guarantee. |\n| DP09 | Seven authored native value shapes: Date, odd ArrayBuffer, offset Uint8Array/DataView, BigInt64Array, Blob and nested array. Unrelated scalar update, peer, export/import, fresh descriptor; insert/import rejection at first/middle/last nested function; raw row/version rollback and valid suffix. | No post-submission mutation, custom prototypes, cycles, Map/Set, detached/shared/resizable buffers or schema transforms in this companion. |\n| DP10 | Six legal database/store substitutions, prefix neighbor and anchor; scalar typed keys in separate transactions, update/import/clear, peer/public/durable/restore and untouched version values. | Native receiving covers prototype/Unicode creation and ordinary writes, not the entire controlled renaming/import/clear matrix. |\n| DP17 | Tarball ESM/CJS consumers plus missing-export controls; Vite with no aliases/config rescue; native browser execution of persist/reopen. | Installed dependency closure is pinned with tarball overrides. This does not prove registry range selection, old runtimes, all bundlers or every export. |\n\nThe new finite grammars intentionally extend existing models without multiplying\nall value shapes by the entire lifecycle state space. Existing fixed/random\nhistory campaigns and direct replay remain unchanged. The TLA+ lifecycle models\nare unchanged because names, payload fidelity and package resolution add no\nlifecycle or authority policy.\n\n## Sensitivity and test-first evidence\n\n[`review-evidence/donor-port`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/review-evidence/donor-port/) contains the\nreplay script, source hash, classifications and complete receipts.\n\n- `name-prefix`: change exact metadata-store matching to `startsWith`.\n  Six assertion failures at metadata ownership after replacement; a neighbor's\n  entries disappear. This is neither timeout nor setup failure.\n- `premature-readiness`: publish readiness before persisted restore.\n  Four assertions reject `ready` while native reads remain held/pending.\n- `json-values`: JSON-convert accepted rows before native persistence.\n  The Date history rejects the peer's string value at the first insert checkpoint.\n- Two resolver controls remove one installed tarball entry each. ESM/CJS loaders\n  fail with the missing-entry path; workspace aliases cannot rescue either path.\n- Value-observer controls accept each correctly constructed corpus value and\n  reject flattened shapes, lost view offsets, altered backing bytes and omissions.\n\nAll production mutants were restored. These establish sensitivity to the named\nwrong designs, not universal coverage. There is no newly repaired source bug.\n\nThe first legal-name draft inserted numeric `0` and string `\"0\"` in one Collection\ntransaction and reproduced the already tracked HC005 core payload collision.\nBoth ordinary and unusual names lost the numeric row. The final name grammar uses\nseparate transactions to isolate routing; HC005 stays open with its existing core\nowner rather than being represented as fixed by this port.\n\n## Native provider distinction\n\nChromium and Firefox receive all seven preservation shapes. The local Playwright\nWebKit provider rejects Blob preparation with native `UnknownError: Error preparing\nBlob/File data to be stored in object store`. A direct native transaction without\nadapter code reproduces it. The executable raw-provider witness preserves this\nclassification.\n\nFor the two Blob-containing WebKit cells, the test requires that exact native\nfailure, matching adapter rejection, empty public/durable/export rows, and a\nsuccessful Date suffix. These are executed rejection tests, not skipped tests or\nBlob-preservation evidence. If native Blob storage succeeds on another platform,\nthe same test executes the full preservation history. A Blob-capable WebKit\nreceiving witness is still needed for that claim; the coverage map names it.\n\n## Guide audit\n\n| Requirement | Evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Existing persistence, schema, readiness and per-store contracts; each owner states its finite domain and exclusions. No recovery/ownership policy is inferred from a donor. |\n| ORC-002 | Authored per-store arrays and tagged value descriptions never derive expected rows from production storage or version classifiers. Production receives detached scalar inputs or fresh native values. |\n| ORC-003 | Opening and adjacent prose distinguish law, value model, finite grammar, real API driver and checkpoint in each oracle/companion. |\n| ORC-004 | No new random generator: bounded enumeration is explicit. Removing unusual names loses dictionary/routing cases; removing independent names loses isolation; removing native holds loses premature-readiness reach; removing offsets/types loses fidelity. Empty/reserved stores and conflicting declarations remain separate negative controls. |\n| ORC-005 | Native open admission counts precede settlement; zero old versions prove absent names; held native reads precede readiness. Public/peer/raw/export/restore observations occur at stated cuts. Package consumers execute real loaders and storage. |\n| ORC-006 | Three production mutants fail by assertion at the named cuts; value and packed resolver controls remain executable. |\n| ORC-007 | Not triggered for new finite enumerations. Existing generated persistence and cross-tab campaigns remain registered and unchanged. |\n| ORC-008 | No lifecycle states added. Value tags, content, offsets and backing bytes remain because an untouched-field observation distinguishes them; object identity is deliberately outside the law. |\n| ORC-009 | Collection status/readiness, persisted restore, public snapshot, native transaction and settlement retain glossary meanings. Value descriptions are test-only observations, not product states. |\n| ORC-010 | Controlled harness retains primary failures and cleanup errors. Native runner aggregates cleanup errors with the primary cause. Tarball fixtures own/dispose temporary directories. No shrinker or failure classifier was changed. |\n| ORC-011 | Native constructors and independent typed/byte observations reject shared JSON assumptions. Direct native Blob admission distinguishes provider rejection from adapter corruption. Legal-name substitution preserves authored meaning. |\n| ORC-012 | This versioned record identifies the reviewed implementation and reports each applicable obligation, calibration and open boundary. No universal bug-class closure is claimed. |\n| ORC-013 | Prefix neighbor versus exact-name metadata; held versus completed restore; offset versus flattened view; native rejected Blob versus accepted Date; existing versus missing packed export are distinguishing neighbors. |\n| ORC-014 | Native first opens, held restore and cross-page values run in Chromium/Firefox/WebKit. WebKit Blob preservation and the wider native naming matrix are explicitly unclaimed, with owners in the coverage map. |\n\n## Validation\n\n- 2,446 adapter runtime/type cases passed; 15 files, no type errors.\n- 192 native browser cases passed across Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. Of the\n  36 added cases, 19 prove rich-value preservation, two prove the documented\n  native Blob rejection/suffix, 12 prove first-open readiness/schema, and three\n  execute packed browser bundles.\n- 16 package-consumer cases passed: 12 existing declaration checks, two packed\n  runtime examples and two missing-export controls.\n- Full test-driver typecheck, changed-file ESLint, formatting and whitespace\n  checks passed. No production code changed, so no core rerun is claimed.\n\nAn attempted dependency refresh hit the configured registry's unavailable\npackages. Tests used the available cached Vite/Rollup installation; source,\nmanifest and lockfile were not changed to bypass it. A dependency declaration\nremoved by its prepare script was restored from the local package cache; external\npacking now uses `npm pack --ignore-scripts`. No suite rebuilds another suite's\ninput. The native CI build list now includes the adapter before packed-browser\nconsumption.\n\n## What remains\n\n- Product decisions: abnormal native closure (DP02), mutable-value capture and\n  ownership (DP12), and worker-host support (DP13). These have not been silently\n  assigned recovery behavior.\n- Optional new APIs: blocked-operation diagnostics, atomic storage read-modify-write,\n  and durability hints. Existing accepted contracts remain unchanged.\n- Provider evidence: WebKit Blob preservation and the wider native naming matrix.\n- Existing open boundaries, including HC005 and the previously recorded lifecycle/\n  suspension/crash limits, remain in the coverage map. This port does not retire them.\n\n\n## Approved follow-up\n\nThe later [follow-up audit](2026-10-06-indexeddb-donor-followup.md) records the\napproved closure/capture/worker contracts, optional blocked diagnostics, core\ntyped-key repair, and completed native naming and persistent-WebKit Blob evidence.\nThe earlier findings above retain their original revision and evidence boundary.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# IndexedDB donor coverage port\n\nReviewed implementation: `c4441632c7ac595d11c57061f5c5f9a3ae556de9`.\nProduction baseline: `fd3c05c818d76489438224e24d4e935751596dc0`.\nThis record concerns DP08, DP09, DP10 and DP17 from the\n[sighted donor survey](2026-10-06-indexeddb-donor-survey.md).\nIt preserves the survey's original dispositions as historical evidence.\n\n## Result and receiving scope\n\nNo production changes or new product policy were required. The port extends the\nexisting per-store and persistence models and adds a separate distribution smoke\nlane. The shared rich-value definition is `structured-clone-oracle.ts`; its\ncontrolled receiving companion is `persistence-values-oracle.test.ts`. The native\ndriver is `e2e/coverage-browser.ts`, with comparisons in `value-oracle.spec.ts`.\nDrivers are not counted as additional independent models.\n\n| Finding | Executed law and bound | Limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| DP08 | Two/three competing first native opens; same/independent absent names; complete identical declarations; schema, held restore/readiness, disjoint writes, convergence, fresh restore and later upgrade. Native engines receive three-open histories. | Opens settle before writes; no callback-order promise or union of conflicting declarations. No new liveness guarantee. |\n| DP09 | Seven authored native value shapes: Date, odd ArrayBuffer, offset Uint8Array/DataView, BigInt64Array, Blob and nested array. Unrelated scalar update, peer, export/import, fresh descriptor; insert/import rejection at first/middle/last nested function; raw row/version rollback and valid suffix. | No post-submission mutation, custom prototypes, cycles, Map/Set, detached/shared/resizable buffers or schema transforms in this companion. |\n| DP10 | Six legal database/store substitutions, prefix neighbor and anchor; scalar typed keys in separate transactions, update/import/clear, peer/public/durable/restore and untouched version values. | Native receiving covers prototype/Unicode creation and ordinary writes, not the entire controlled renaming/import/clear matrix. |\n| DP17 | Tarball ESM/CJS consumers plus missing-export controls; Vite with no aliases/config rescue; native browser execution of persist/reopen. | Installed dependency closure is pinned with tarball overrides. This does not prove registry range selection, old runtimes, all bundlers or every export. |\n\nThe new finite grammars intentionally extend existing models without multiplying\nall value shapes by the entire lifecycle state space. Existing fixed/random\nhistory campaigns and direct replay remain unchanged. The TLA+ lifecycle models\nare unchanged because names, payload fidelity and package resolution add no\nlifecycle or authority policy.\n\n## Sensitivity and test-first evidence\n\n[`review-evidence/donor-port`](https://github.com/TanStack/db/tree/main/review-evidence/donor-port/) contains the\nreplay script, source hash, classifications and complete receipts.\n\n- `name-prefix`: change exact metadata-store matching to `startsWith`.\n  Six assertion failures at metadata ownership after replacement; a neighbor's\n  entries disappear. This is neither timeout nor setup failure.\n- `premature-readiness`: publish readiness before persisted restore.\n  Four assertions reject `ready` while native reads remain held/pending.\n- `json-values`: JSON-convert accepted rows before native persistence.\n  The Date history rejects the peer's string value at the first insert checkpoint.\n- Two resolver controls remove one installed tarball entry each. ESM/CJS loaders\n  fail with the missing-entry path; workspace aliases cannot rescue either path.\n- Value-observer controls accept each correctly constructed corpus value and\n  reject flattened shapes, lost view offsets, altered backing bytes and omissions.\n\nAll production mutants were restored. These establish sensitivity to the named\nwrong designs, not universal coverage. There is no newly repaired source bug.\n\nThe first legal-name draft inserted numeric `0` and string `\"0\"` in one Collection\ntransaction and reproduced the already tracked HC005 core payload collision.\nBoth ordinary and unusual names lost the numeric row. The final name grammar uses\nseparate transactions to isolate routing; HC005 stays open with its existing core\nowner rather than being represented as fixed by this port.\n\n## Native provider distinction\n\nChromium and Firefox receive all seven preservation shapes. The local Playwright\nWebKit provider rejects Blob preparation with native `UnknownError: Error preparing\nBlob/File data to be stored in object store`. A direct native transaction without\nadapter code reproduces it. The executable raw-provider witness preserves this\nclassification.\n\nFor the two Blob-containing WebKit cells, the test requires that exact native\nfailure, matching adapter rejection, empty public/durable/export rows, and a\nsuccessful Date suffix. These are executed rejection tests, not skipped tests or\nBlob-preservation evidence. If native Blob storage succeeds on another platform,\nthe same test executes the full preservation history. A Blob-capable WebKit\nreceiving witness is still needed for that claim; the coverage map names it.\n\n## Guide audit\n\n| Requirement | Evidence |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | Existing persistence, schema, readiness and per-store contracts; each owner states its finite domain and exclusions. No recovery/ownership policy is inferred from a donor. |\n| ORC-002 | Authored per-store arrays and tagged value descriptions never derive expected rows from production storage or version classifiers. Production receives detached scalar inputs or fresh native values. |\n| ORC-003 | Opening and adjacent prose distinguish law, value model, finite grammar, real API driver and checkpoint in each oracle/companion. |\n| ORC-004 | No new random generator: bounded enumeration is explicit. Removing unusual names loses dictionary/routing cases; removing independent names loses isolation; removing native holds loses premature-readiness reach; removing offsets/types loses fidelity. Empty/reserved stores and conflicting declarations remain separate negative controls. |\n| ORC-005 | Native open admission counts precede settlement; zero old versions prove absent names; held native reads precede readiness. Public/peer/raw/export/restore observations occur at stated cuts. Package consumers execute real loaders and storage. |\n| ORC-006 | Three production mutants fail by assertion at the named cuts; value and packed resolver controls remain executable. |\n| ORC-007 | Not triggered for new finite enumerations. Existing generated persistence and cross-tab campaigns remain registered and unchanged. |\n| ORC-008 | No lifecycle states added. Value tags, content, offsets and backing bytes remain because an untouched-field observation distinguishes them; object identity is deliberately outside the law. |\n| ORC-009 | Collection status/readiness, persisted restore, public snapshot, native transaction and settlement retain glossary meanings. Value descriptions are test-only observations, not product states. |\n| ORC-010 | Controlled harness retains primary failures and cleanup errors. Native runner aggregates cleanup errors with the primary cause. Tarball fixtures own/dispose temporary directories. No shrinker or failure classifier was changed. |\n| ORC-011 | Native constructors and independent typed/byte observations reject shared JSON assumptions. Direct native Blob admission distinguishes provider rejection from adapter corruption. Legal-name substitution preserves authored meaning. |\n| ORC-012 | This versioned record identifies the reviewed implementation and reports each applicable obligation, calibration and open boundary. No universal bug-class closure is claimed. |\n| ORC-013 | Prefix neighbor versus exact-name metadata; held versus completed restore; offset versus flattened view; native rejected Blob versus accepted Date; existing versus missing packed export are distinguishing neighbors. |\n| ORC-014 | Native first opens, held restore and cross-page values run in Chromium/Firefox/WebKit. WebKit Blob preservation and the wider native naming matrix are explicitly unclaimed, with owners in the coverage map. |\n\n## Validation\n\n- 2,446 adapter runtime/type cases passed; 15 files, no type errors.\n- 192 native browser cases passed across Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. Of the\n  36 added cases, 19 prove rich-value preservation, two prove the documented\n  native Blob rejection/suffix, 12 prove first-open readiness/schema, and three\n  execute packed browser bundles.\n- 16 package-consumer cases passed: 12 existing declaration checks, two packed\n  runtime examples and two missing-export controls.\n- Full test-driver typecheck, changed-file ESLint, formatting and whitespace\n  checks passed. No production code changed, so no core rerun is claimed.\n\nAn attempted dependency refresh hit the configured registry's unavailable\npackages. Tests used the available cached Vite/Rollup installation; source,\nmanifest and lockfile were not changed to bypass it. A dependency declaration\nremoved by its prepare script was restored from the local package cache; external\npacking now uses `npm pack --ignore-scripts`. No suite rebuilds another suite's\ninput. The native CI build list now includes the adapter before packed-browser\nconsumption.\n\n## What remains\n\n- Product decisions: abnormal native closure (DP02), mutable-value capture and\n  ownership (DP12), and worker-host support (DP13). These have not been silently\n  assigned recovery behavior.\n- Optional new APIs: blocked-operation diagnostics, atomic storage read-modify-write,\n  and durability hints. Existing accepted contracts remain unchanged.\n- Provider evidence: WebKit Blob preservation and the wider native naming matrix.\n- Existing open boundaries, including HC005 and the previously recorded lifecycle/\n  suspension/crash limits, remain in the coverage map. This port does not retire them.\n\n\n## Approved follow-up\n\nThe later [follow-up audit](2026-10-06-indexeddb-donor-followup.md) records the\napproved closure/capture/worker contracts, optional blocked diagnostics, core\ntyped-key repair, and completed native naming and persistent-WebKit Blob evidence.\nThe earlier findings above retain their original revision and evidence boundary.\n"}
